{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>That which was.</b> The poet said concerning the upper creations, 'He commanded and they were created,' and He established them forever, to eternity; and concerning the lower creations he said, for His name alone is exalted. The meaning is that all of them are as though naught. And the meaning of 'that which was' concerns the spheres and their hosts, for they are like circles turning round and round, and their beginning is as their end and their end as their beginning." "<b>And that which is done is what shall be done.</b> Concerning the universals, which guard the form of man and the form of horse and the form of every living thing and the form of every plant; and the offspring of these move through the motions of the upper ones. And if the upper ones stand, the universals stand, for they are like the pattern of the array of the hosts. And the meaning is: even though I cannot count the particulars, their universals are guarded and known and counted. And in this way you will see that the world above and the world below stand on a single path, and there is nothing new."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The phrase 'כי נשגב שמו לבדו' appears to quote Psalm 148:13; its logical connection to the lower creations is left unstated by Ibn Ezra and preserved as is.", 1 "Reading שתבנית as a contraction of שתי בנית ('two patterns' or 'like the pattern'); the text is slightly awkward and may be corrupt."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:26:51.022092653Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}